**Read:** [The fluid mechanics of aerosols — Goodarz Ahmadi, Clarkson](../sources/Fluid-Mechanics-of-Aerosols-Ahmadi.lingua#r_175C6Q2GTKPZJJ6XSRHF5979G0), Coagulation of Particles in full; [Aerosols and coagulation — Chiang C. Mei, MIT](../sources/Aerosols-and-Coagulation-Mei.lingua#r_433KSJ2E60YP29TNWRQV1Z7PS9), the full diffusion-to-a-sphere derivation and appendices.

When two particles join, one combined object replaces two separate objects. Total particle count decreases while material mass can remain unchanged. A counter's size bins can change even if no material leaves the observed volume. Fragmentation produces the opposite kind of count change.

Distinguish approaching, colliding, sticking and coalescing. A geometrical encounter may not create a durable aggregate; a joined pair need not immediately become a compact sphere. A collision kernel describes encounters under chosen transport assumptions, while an efficiency can represent the probability that an encounter gives the modeled outcome.

**Work:** Track both number and material mass through five explicitly listed pair-merging events. Construct the size-bin counts before and after using a stated compact-sphere approximation, then explain which conclusion changes if the products remain porous aggregates.

**Return:** [Clean manufacturing and contamination control — from familiar objects to controlled processes](../Clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control.lingua#r_7D8SQ8BVQDZQBDBGC6DP0JAMZN).
